YANI: 'Fearsomeness'
This one is a little unpolished, and it would mean making major changes
to the way the game works, but I think with a little help we can beat
it into a really interesting idea to improve the game.

I propose adding a new player stat to the game: For want of a better
term, I'll call it 'Fearsomeness'. Like luck, it would not be visible
to the player except through enlightentment, which would give messages
like "You are fearsome", "you are terrifying", "you are awesome", "you
are pathetic", "you are pitiful". Monsters (including pets) would have
their own fearsomeness scores, which rely on a similar combination of
level, strength and current HP.

The point of fearsomeness is to make monsters run away from you: If
your fearsomeness score is above that of the monster, it runs away.
This means that monsters will still 'turn to flee' when wounded, but
that they may well keep fighting for longer if you are low on HP. It
also means that underpowered monsters may take one look at you and run
before even trying to fight. This is fine. As well as being a useful
strategy for many players, it serves other purposes which will be
explained later. The fear will eventually wear off, and the monster
will return to try again- it could well be that by the time it returns
the conditions may have changed and it will be able to attack. Note
that some monsters (most undead, named monsters, stupid monsters etc)
would be unnaffected by fear. It should also be impossible (or at least
very difficult) to tame any monster who is afraid of you.

Fearsomeness is calculated by adding the character's strength,
constitution and XPlevel, then multiplying the whole lot by X. X is the
proportion of the player's HP to maxHP, so a player at 25/50 HP will
have X=0.5. This means that tough, strong players will look more
fearsome than newbie dweebs fresh off the Dlvl1 stairs, but any player
will look more vulnerable and become less fearsome as they are wounded.
There are other modifiers to fearsomeness as well:

-Being paralysed, trapped (ie beartrap, held by monster) or asleep
reduces your fearsomeness dramatically.
-Being confused reduces it slightly.
-Being blind reduces it slightly, but only until you get telepathy.
-This is the important one: Groups of monsters standing near to one
another draw fearsomeness from each other: so while that one newt on
his own might be scared of you, he would be quite happy to attack if he
was standing in the shadow of the dragon currently biting chunks out of
your backside. Or one grid bug might be afraid, but ten grid bugs will
not. Likewise, your kitten might not want to take on the watch captain
alone, but backed up by two housecats, she might well chance it.
-Riding a large steed (warhorse, dragon) will make you more fearsome.
-Wielding a silver weapon will make you more fearsome to silver-haters.
-Wielding foobane will make you more fearsome to foo monsters.
-Wearing a complete set of Elven armour (shield, hat, mithril, boots,
cloak) will make you more fearsome to Orcs, and vice versa.
-Chaotic characters can increase their fearsomeness by wearing black.
-Lawful characters can be more fearsome by wearing white.
-Wizards gain fearsomeness by wearing robes, cornuthaums. They lose it
for wearing bulky armour.
-Knight and valks are more fearsome when dressed in heavy armour.
-Barbarians look most awesome when half-naked and flexing.
-Rogues are most fearsome when stealthy and dressed in black.
-Healers are at their most awesome when dressed in white or green.
-Wielding a weapon in which you are "expert" is pretty fearsome.
-Casting "cause fear" at a monster increases its fear of you.
-Elbereth under your feet increases your fearsomeness considerably to
all Elbereth-respecting monsters.
-Ditto for scroll of scare monster.

Note that the above implies a change to the behaviour of Elbereth,
scare monster and cause fear. This would make these methods a little
less reliable, especially for wounded or wimpy players facing
overwhelming odds. Elbereth would have to give a *very* significant
boost to fearsomeness.

What's the point of this patch?
-Lone monsters who pose no threat to the player will keep their
distance until they have enough support and then attack.
-It allows pets to 'gang up' on larger monsters.
-It reduces the predictability of monster behaviour, in particular when
faced with Elbereth/ ?oSM.
-it encourages players to dress appropriately for their class and
alignment.
-it gives pacifist players another survival tool. (Albeit a
double-edged one)
-it makes the foobane weapons a little more powerful.
-it makes riding a little more useful.

Like I say, it's not perfect, but I'm sure with some work it could be
viable. What does anyone think?



